<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  IIS configuration for the KSOU Online build, deployed to /ksou_test/.

  This file lives in public/, so Vite copies it verbatim into dist/ and it
  ships at the root of the deployed folder. It applies to /ksou_test/ and
  everything under it, and does NOT affect the university's existing site at
  the domain root — an IIS web.config only governs its own folder downward.

  It does two things, both of which the site is broken without.

  ── 1. MIME types ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  IIS refuses to serve a file extension it has no MIME mapping for: the
  request returns 404, even though the file is sitting right there on disk.
  Most IIS installations have no mapping for .webp, and older ones none for
  .woff2 either.

  This is the real cause of the .webp 404s, and it is worth being precise
  about, because it is easy to misdiagnose: the problem is the *extension*,
  not the folder. Moving those images from src/assets/ into public/ does not
  fix it — the file is still called .webp and IIS still will not serve it.
  Registering the type here does fix it, wherever the file lives.

  That matters for fonts as well as images. This project self-hosts DM Sans
  and DM Serif Display, which Vite bundles as .woff2; without the mapping
  below every page renders in a fallback system font.

  <remove> precedes each <mimeMap> on purpose. If a mapping already exists
  at server or site level, adding a duplicate makes IIS return HTTP 500 for
  every request in this folder. Removing first is idempotent and safe.

  ── 2. SPA fallback ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  This is a single-page app: /ksou_test/contact is a client-side route, not
  a file on disk. Without a fallback, a visitor who types that URL, refreshes
  the page, or follows a shared link gets an IIS 404. Clicking through from
  the homepage would work, which is exactly what makes this bug ship — it is
  invisible during casual testing. (It is the same failure vercel.json's
  rewrite was added to fix on the previous host.)

  The two conditions are what keep pre-rendering intact. `npm run build`
  writes real HTML to dist/about/index.html and so on for all 27 routes, and
  those are physical directories — so IsDirectory matches, the rule does not
  fire, and IIS serves the pre-rendered file through its default document.
  Only genuinely nonexistent paths fall through to index.html.

  ⚠ REQUIRES THE IIS URL REWRITE MODULE. It is not installed by default. If
  it is missing, IIS returns HTTP 500.19 for every request in this folder
  rather than ignoring the section — so if the whole site 500s immediately
  after deployment, install URL Rewrite (or delete the <rewrite> block,
  accepting that deep links will 404).
-->
<configuration>
  <system.webServer>

    <staticContent>
      <remove fileExtension=".webp" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".webp" mimeType="image/webp" />

      <remove fileExtension=".woff2" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".woff2" mimeType="font/woff2" />

      <remove fileExtension=".woff" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".woff" mimeType="font/woff" />

      <remove fileExtension=".json" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".json" mimeType="application/json" />

      <remove fileExtension=".svg" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".svg" mimeType="image/svg+xml" />

      <!--
        The PDFs (prospectus, academic calendar, question papers, notices)
        must be served as application/pdf with no Content-Disposition, or
        they download instead of opening in the browser's viewer. That
        open-in-a-new-tab behaviour is deliberate and matches how the main
        KSOU site serves the same documents.
      -->
      <remove fileExtension=".pdf" />
      <mimeMap fileExtension=".pdf" mimeType="application/pdf" />
    </staticContent>

    <rewrite>
      <rules>
        <rule name="KSOU Online SPA fallback" stopProcessing="true">
          <match url=".*" />
          <conditions logicalGrouping="MatchAll">
            <!-- Never rewrite something that exists: assets, PDFs, and the
                 pre-rendered route folders all resolve on their own. -->
            <add input="{REQUEST_FILENAME}" matchType="IsFile" negate="true" />
            <add input="{REQUEST_FILENAME}" matchType="IsDirectory" negate="true" />
          </conditions>
          <action type="Rewrite" url="/ksou_test/index.html" />
        </rule>
      </rules>
    </rewrite>

  </system.webServer>
</configuration>
